## _app.tsx

Every page in the Pages Router is wrapped by `_app.tsx`. The default implementation just renders the page:

```tsx
// pages/_app.tsx (default)
import type { AppProps } from "next/app";

export default function App({ Component, pageProps }: AppProps) {
  return <Component {...pageProps} />;
}
```

You customise it to add anything that should be present on every page:

```tsx
// pages/_app.tsx — full example
import type { AppProps } from "next/app";
import { SessionProvider } from "next-auth/react";
import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from "@tanstack/react-query";
import "../styles/globals.css"; // Global CSS must be imported here
import { Layout } from "../components/layout";

const queryClient = new QueryClient();

export default function App({ Component, pageProps: { session, ...pageProps } }: AppProps) {
  return (
    <SessionProvider session={session}>
      <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>
        <Layout>
          <Component {...pageProps} />
        </Layout>
      </QueryClientProvider>
    </SessionProvider>
  );
}
```

Key things `_app.tsx` is responsible for:

- Global CSS imports (only place you can import global CSS in Pages Router)
- Provider mounting (Redux, auth, theme, query client)
- Persistent layout (navigation, footer, sidebars)
- Error boundaries for the whole app
- `getInitialProps` for app-level data fetching (rare, has downsides)

## _document.tsx

`_document.tsx` customises the actual HTML document structure. It renders server-side only — never in the browser:

```tsx
// pages/_document.tsx
import { Html, Head, Main, NextScript } from "next/document";

export default function Document() {
  return (
    <Html lang="en">
      <Head>
        {/* Fonts, preconnect, meta tags that apply globally */}
        <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
        <link
          href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter&display=swap"
          rel="stylesheet"
        />
        <meta name="theme-color" content="#0070f3" />
      </Head>
      <body className="bg-white">
        <Main />
        <NextScript />
      </body>
    </Html>
  );
}
```

The four components from `next/document` are required in the positions shown — they inject the page content and Next.js scripts correctly.

## What goes where

| Need | `_app.tsx` | `_document.tsx` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Global CSS | ✅ | ❌ |
| Providers | ✅ | ❌ |
| Persistent layout | ✅ | ❌ |
| Custom `<html>` / `<body>` | ❌ | ✅ |
| Global `<meta>` / `<link>` | ❌ | ✅ |
| Blocking scripts | ❌ | ✅ (use sparingly) |
| Event handlers, hooks | ✅ | ❌ (server only) |

## App Router equivalents

Both files are replaced by `app/layout.tsx`:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx — replaces _app.tsx + _document.tsx
import "./globals.css";
import { Providers } from "./providers";
import { Inter } from "next/font/google";

const inter = Inter({ subsets: ["latin"] });

export default function RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <html lang="en" className={inter.className}>
      <body>
        <Providers>{children}</Providers>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

The root layout must export `<html>` and `<body>` (what `_document.tsx` used to do), and it's also where you mount providers (what `_app.tsx` used to do). One file, one concept.

## Migration note

When migrating from Pages to App Router, the tricky part is that you can run both routers in parallel. During that window:

- Global CSS imported in `_app.tsx` doesn't apply to App Router pages
- Providers in `_app.tsx` don't wrap App Router pages
- You need parallel implementations in both `_app.tsx` and `app/layout.tsx`

The last pages route to be ported is when you can delete `_app.tsx` and `_document.tsx` entirely.

## Why it exists

Statically generated pages are fast because they're pre-rendered at build time. The downside: content editors can't preview unpublished changes until a new build runs. For a large site that builds every few minutes, that's a frustrating delay.

Draft Mode solves it by making Next.js skip the cache for a specific browser session:

```
Normal request →  served from Full Route Cache (stale-until-revalidate)
Draft request  →  bypass cache → fetch from CMS live API each time
```

## Setup

### Enable endpoint

```ts
// app/api/draft/enable/route.ts
import { draftMode } from "next/headers";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";

export async function GET(request: Request) {
  const { searchParams } = new URL(request.url);
  const secret = searchParams.get("secret");
  const slug = searchParams.get("slug");

  // Validate secret token
  if (secret !== process.env.DRAFT_SECRET) {
    return new Response("Invalid secret", { status: 401 });
  }

  // Enable draft mode — sets a cookie
  const draft = await draftMode();
  draft.enable();

  // Redirect to the content being previewed
  redirect(`/blog/${slug}`);
}
```

### Disable endpoint

```ts
// app/api/draft/disable/route.ts
import { draftMode } from "next/headers";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";

export async function GET() {
  const draft = await draftMode();
  draft.disable();
  redirect("/");
}
```

### Checking draft mode in a page

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
import { draftMode } from "next/headers";
import { getDraftPost, getPublishedPost } from "@/lib/cms";

export default async function BlogPost({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>;
}) {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const { isEnabled } = await draftMode();

  // Fetch the appropriate version
  const post = isEnabled
    ? await getDraftPost(slug)   // live CMS draft API
    : await getPublishedPost(slug); // cached published content

  return (
    <article>
      {isEnabled && (
        <div className="bg-yellow-100 p-2 text-sm">
          Draft Mode — <a href="/api/draft/disable">Exit preview</a>
        </div>
      )}
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <div>{post.body}</div>
    </article>
  );
}
```

## CMS integration flow

1. Content editor writes a draft in Contentful / Sanity / Payload
2. Clicks "Preview" in the CMS UI
3. CMS fires `https://yoursite.com/api/draft/enable?secret=abc&slug=my-post`
4. Route Handler validates the secret and sets the Draft Mode cookie
5. Browser redirects to `/blog/my-post`
6. The page sees `isEnabled = true` and fetches from the draft API
7. Editor reviews, makes changes, the page updates on refresh
8. Editor publishes in the CMS, clicks "Exit preview"
9. Draft Mode cookie is cleared; page reverts to cached content

## Protecting the endpoint

The secret prevents anyone from arbitrarily bypassing your cache:

```ts
// .env.local
DRAFT_SECRET=a-long-random-string-nobody-can-guess
```

The CMS is given this secret out-of-band. Your endpoint validates it before enabling Draft Mode. Without validation, anyone could hit `/api/draft/enable` and force your production site to fetch from the draft API.

## Pages Router — Preview Mode

The Pages Router equivalent uses `setPreviewData` / `clearPreviewData` in an API route:

```ts
// pages/api/preview.ts
export default function handler(req, res) {
  if (req.query.secret !== process.env.DRAFT_SECRET) {
    return res.status(401).json({ message: "Invalid token" });
  }
  res.setPreviewData({});
  res.redirect(req.query.slug);
}
```

Same concept, older API — `draftMode()` in App Router is the cleaner equivalent.

## XSS — cross-site scripting

React escapes content by default — JSX expressions are safe:

```tsx
// ✅ Safe — React escapes this
const userInput = "<script>alert('xss')</script>";
return <p>{userInput}</p>;
// Renders as text: <script>alert('xss')</script>
```

The danger is `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` — only use it with sanitised HTML:

```tsx
// ❌ Dangerous — raw user content in innerHTML
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: userPost.content }} />

// ✅ Sanitise first
import DOMPurify from "isomorphic-dompurify";

<div
  dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{
    __html: DOMPurify.sanitize(userPost.content, {
      ALLOWED_TAGS: ["p", "strong", "em", "a", "ul", "ol", "li"],
    }),
  }}
/>
```

## Environment variable leakage

```
# ❌ Secret in NEXT_PUBLIC_ — ends up in client bundle
NEXT_PUBLIC_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:secret@host/db

# ✅ Server-only
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:secret@host/db
```

Audit this in CI — search for secrets in the production bundle:

```bash
# Check built bundle for known sensitive strings
grep -r "sk_live" .next/static/
```

Better: validate your env schema at startup (with Zod) and fail the build if a required server variable is accidentally prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_`.

## CSRF — cross-site request forgery

Next.js Server Actions are CSRF-protected by default — they require the request to come from the same origin (Origin header is validated).

For Route Handlers handling mutations (POST, DELETE), validate Origin yourself:

```ts
// app/api/sensitive-action/route.ts
export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const origin = request.headers.get("origin");
  if (origin !== process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL) {
    return new Response("Forbidden", { status: 403 });
  }
  // proceed
}
```

Alternatively, require a CSRF token in the request body and validate it against a server-stored value.

## Content Security Policy

```ts
// next.config.ts
const cspHeader = [
  "default-src 'self'",
  "script-src 'self' 'unsafe-eval' 'unsafe-inline'", // loosen for dev, tighten for prod
  "style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'",
  "img-src 'self' blob: data:",
  "font-src 'self'",
  "connect-src 'self'",
  "frame-ancestors 'none'",
].join("; ");

export default {
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: "/(.*)",
        headers: [
          { key: "Content-Security-Policy", value: cspHeader },
          { key: "X-Frame-Options", value: "DENY" },
          { key: "X-Content-Type-Options", value: "nosniff" },
          { key: "Referrer-Policy", value: "strict-origin-when-cross-origin" },
          {
            key: "Permissions-Policy",
            value: "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()",
          },
        ],
      },
    ];
  },
};
```

Use [https://csp-evaluator.withgoogle.com](https://csp-evaluator.withgoogle.com) to evaluate your policy.

## Rate limiting

Enforce in middleware so it runs before any route logic:

```ts
// middleware.ts
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";
import type { NextRequest } from "next/server";

const rateLimitMap = new Map<string, { count: number; reset: number }>();

export function middleware(request: NextRequest) {
  if (!request.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith("/api")) {
    return NextResponse.next();
  }

  const ip = request.ip ?? "unknown";
  const now = Date.now();
  const windowMs = 60_000; // 1 minute
  const limit = 100;

  const current = rateLimitMap.get(ip);
  if (!current || now > current.reset) {
    rateLimitMap.set(ip, { count: 1, reset: now + windowMs });
  } else if (current.count >= limit) {
    return new NextResponse("Too Many Requests", { status: 429 });
  } else {
    current.count++;
  }

  return NextResponse.next();
}
```

For production, back the rate limit store with Redis (in-memory doesn't survive restarts or scale across instances).

## Dependency vulnerabilities

```bash
# In CI
pnpm audit --audit-level=high
```

Block deployments if high-severity vulnerabilities are found. Use Dependabot or Renovate for automated dependency updates.

## Server Action security

Server Actions are public endpoints — treat them like API routes:

```ts
"use server";
import { auth } from "@/auth";
import { z } from "zod";

const schema = z.object({
  title: z.string().min(1).max(200),
  body: z.string().min(1),
});

export async function createPost(formData: FormData) {
  // 1. Authenticate
  const session = await auth();
  if (!session?.user) throw new Error("Unauthenticated");

  // 2. Validate
  const parsed = schema.safeParse({
    title: formData.get("title"),
    body: formData.get("body"),
  });
  if (!parsed.success) throw new Error("Invalid input");

  // 3. Act
  await db.post.create({
    data: { ...parsed.data, authorId: session.user.id },
  });
}
```

Never trust formData without parsing and validating — anyone can send arbitrary values to a Server Action URL.
